---
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { routeUrl } from "../lib/url.ts";

/* Both hosts serve /404.html for an unmatched path, so this needs no route
   configuration, only to exist. It is read more attentively than most pages
   here: the reader has stopped and is looking at the address bar. */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Not found"
  description="A well-formed address that refers to nothing."
>
  <PageIntro
    kind="page"
    eyebrow="404"
    title="This address is perfectly well formed, and refers to nothing."
  >
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-xs">
      <Button href={routeUrl("/")} tone="primary" size="control">Home</Button>
      <Button href={routeUrl("/blog")} tone="secondary" size="control">Blog</Button>
      <Button href={routeUrl("/docs")} tone="secondary" size="control">Docs</Button>
    </div>
  </PageIntro>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Russell ran into this in 1905. &ldquo;The present King of France&rdquo; is
      grammatical, unambiguous, and built entirely from words that mean things.
      It still picks out nobody. A name can be in perfect working order with
      nothing on the other end of it, and a sentence containing one is not
      thereby nonsense. It is simply false.
    </p>
    <p>
      Whether anything was ever here, a 404 does not say. There is a separate
      code for that stronger claim, 410 Gone, and it is vanishingly rare
      because knowing a thing is absent costs far more than failing to find it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Probably a typo, and not necessarily yours. If a link on this site sent
      you here, that is my bug: the <a href={routeUrl("/about")}>about page</a>
      says how to reach me.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
